WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.860
Film Courage: When I look back at some of
the Danny Elfman videos did you help direct

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:05.860
any of those?

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.080
Richard Elfman (Filmmaker of FORBIDDEN ZONE):
Yes, I’ve helped produced some, directed

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.150
one (PRIVATE LIFE) although it was Danny’s
conception.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.429
He knows very much what he wants in the videos.

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:15.759
Film Courage: They are very creative.

00:00:15.759 --> 00:00:22.880
And do you see a correlation…or not a correlation
but a difference in music videos from the

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.580
80’s and 90’s compared to now?

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.880
In terms of the production value wasn’t
there as much but the story was.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.650
Richard: Well a story is very important in
a musical number or a video.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:40.690
Great videos have a beginning, middle and
end (so do great musical numbers) going back

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:47.280
to CHICAGO which had great serviceable music
but it had beautifully choreographed dance

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.600
numbers that had a beginning, middle and end.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.870
Extraordinary, extraordinary.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:01:00.520
Okay MOULIN ROUGE! unfortunately what they
did was they shot tons of footage for the

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.690
musical numbers and then just shot, shot,
shot, shot, shot.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.530
No beginning, middle and end.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.550
You are bored by the end of the musical number.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.670
No story, just a bunch of images.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.759
Film Courage: What did you think of BIRDMAN?

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:22.480
Richard: I liked it and I’m a huge Michael
Keaton fan.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.950
Well I loved him as BATMAN.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:30.790
And I want to see him come back for BEETLEJUICE,
they are talking about doing another BEETLEJUICE.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:35.580
Film Courage: He was great in THE FOUNDER
too (I don’t know if you saw that)?

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.369
About the McDonald’s founder Ray Kroc?

00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:40.060
Richard: I did not see that.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.369
Film Courage: But when you look at BIRDMAN,
is there anything about his character that

00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:46.510
strikes you?

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:52.630
Richard: Well it’s a very different…he’s
one of those actors who can just do anything.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:59.979
It was just an iconic role, not like anything
he has done.

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:06.539
Film Courage: But in terms of…you’ve been
a film director, a theater director was there

00:02:06.539 --> 00:02:12.580
something that got you about the film because
it was not the most happiest of endings?

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.480
Richard: To put it mildly.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:21.130
Film Courage: Without any spoilers but was
there a part that you really resonated with?

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:26.440
Richard: I enjoyed the film but it wasn’t
earthshaking for me.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.660
But I loved the film.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:34.060
I have other films that are my top 10, my
top 20.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.060
Film Courage: Oh!

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.270
Top Ten, I’d love to hear them.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:40.720
Richard: Gosh in a nutshell?

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:57.820
Okay, Hitchcock’s PSYCHO, Scorsese’s CASINO,
what’s the one with RAGING BULL, Coen Brothers

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:15.090
Oh Brother Where Art Thou?, David Lynch BLUE
VELVET, Coppola GODFATHER I and II…you know

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.090
the classics.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:20.340
Film Courage: With Hitchcock I was watching
a documentary and they were talking about

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:26.700
how some of his sort of sexual repression
was able to show…

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.310
Richard: It sure did in PSYCHO!

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:38.330
Film Courage: Right…so what could be a hindrance
to some people actually on screen (who knows

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:44.830
what some of the stories were on set) but
that it really translated into amazing performances.

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:48.950
Just want to hear your take on it since you
seem very open.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.340
Richard: I wouldn’t describe myself as repressed!

00:03:53.340 --> 00:04:00.980
And that’s the thing like how crazy or neurotic
does an artist have to be and how does it

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.730
translate through the work?

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:08.480
I don’t believe you have to be crazy or
neurotic as an artist.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.910
You can learn it in some method acting classes.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.420
I’ve learned tricks to make actors cry.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.620
Film Courage: They [the documentary] talked
about how he didn’t feel comfortable in

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:22.760
his own body and that maybe he even used his
weight as a way to kind of keep people away

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:28.480
from him but then there was something about
that which translated on screen because…

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.160
Richard: I can relate to that because I need
to lose 10 pounds if I want to get into this

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:36.400
senior boxing division next year [Richard
is a former boxer].

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.690
Film Courage: Well I don’t think you need
to lose weight and I wasn’t going there

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.670
but I’m just talking about.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:44.961
Richard: I’m just kidding.

00:04:44.961 --> 00:04:56.540
He was a master and it’s hard to get into
someone’s head and to me that was the ultimate

00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:01.090
explosion of sexual repression.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.540
It was like a perfect film…Oh!

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.510
THE THIRD MAN is one on the top of my list.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.130
That I can watch over and over and over.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:15.840
Film Courage: Do you think there are people
like Orson Welles still in this town today?

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.200
Sort of this charismatic…I mean there are
so many charismatic people but…

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:25.770
Richard: Here is what is going to get me into
trouble, CITIZEN KANE to me is the most overrated

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.070
film of all time.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:28.620
Film Courage: Okay?

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.120
Richard: Brilliant cinematography.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:38.070
I forgot the guy’s name but he actually
shared his director credit with the cinematographer.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:43.240
Film Courage: Not Elia Kazan but…?

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:53.080
Richard: No but Elia Kazan is one of my favorite
directors but getting back to CITIZEN KANE

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:59.770
so okay he takes this brilliant cinematographer
and gives him free reign and does innovative

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:06.040
shots, innovative eating and has a really,
really interesting film.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:11.570
I have a simple test with film being great
or comedy being funny.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:15.090
The film being great is how easy is it for
you to turn it off?

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:18.020
It is that simple, no rocket science.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.530
We’ve all got films that we don’t want
to turn off.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:23.650
We’re late for the appointment and we want
to keep watching.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.060
Those are our greatest films.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:30.520
I’ve never had trouble turning off CITIZEN
KANE.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:38.941
I can’t turn off GODFATHER I or II, I can’t
turn off Scorsese’s greatest films I just

00:06:38.941 --> 00:06:41.460
want to keep watching.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:46.690
I don’t know about you guys but it just
doesn’t hold me?

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:52.750
I love the shots, I love the editing, there
is genius going on but I have no trouble turning

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:53.750
it off.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.900
So it’s not on my top 10 or top 20.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:00.200
Film Courage: How do you feel about TAXI DRIVER?

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.280
Richard: Top 20.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:08.430
Sorry about that, RAGING BULL, TAXI DRIVER,
my gosh!

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.740
So brilliant.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:24.090
And then with modern films I liked WONDER
WOMAN, BLACK PANTHER, and Tim Burton BEETLEJUICE,

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:29.990
EDWARD SCISSOR HANDS and some of my brother’s
greatest work [Danny Elfman] has been for

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.310
Tim Burton.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:33.310
So these are all on my list.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:38.960
Film Courage: What would you say the difference
is between film directors let’s say in the

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:49.110
age of Hitchcock, maybe Scorsese, Coppola
so we are spanning a few decades there compared

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:50.340
to now?

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.601
What’s the difference?

00:07:51.601 --> 00:07:55.770
Is it just the roles that they have to play
by in terms of there is such pressure…

00:07:55.770 --> 00:08:04.900
Richard: I mean every generation has brilliant
filmmakers and a lot of junk that time sorts

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.690
it out, time sorts it out.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:10.610
Film Courage: That’s true because we’re
not really hearing about the ones that fell

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:14.110
by the wayside back then even though it were
less filmmakers.

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:29.139
Richard: Right, we remember Elia Kazan’s
ON THE WATERFRONT or A STREET CAR NAMED DESIRE.

00:08:29.139 --> 00:08:32.510
We think of those but we don’t think about
all the junk that happened in the 1950’s.

